From: Julius Bairaktaris <julius@bairaktaris.de>
Subject: [PATCH] igs: unwind firmware state when a redirect init fails

nss_mirred_init() applies the IGS node and the nexthop to the source physical
port with two synchronous messages, and only afterwards calls nss_ifb_bind(),
which is what sets is_mapped. Either send failing returns -EINVAL with nothing
undone.

That is not recoverable later. Both undo paths, nss_ifb_reset_nexthop() and
nss_ifb_clear_igs_node(), open with a test on is_mapped and return success
having sent nothing when it is clear - and it is clear here, because bind never
ran. A failed send is also not proof the firmware ignored the message: a timeout
means only that no ACK was seen. So a redirect whose init timed out leaves a
nexthop pointing at an IGS node on a physical port, with no host-side record of
it and no way to take it back short of reloading the firmware. The port then
looks perfectly healthy while its traffic goes to a node nothing feeds.

Send the corresponding undo directly on both failure paths, bypassing the
is_mapped gate that exists for the bound case. nss_ifb_clear_config_cb() has to
tolerate a NULL app_data for that, since there is no ifb_info to update yet;
it dereferenced it unconditionally, which would have faulted in softirq.

Signed-off-by: Julius Bairaktaris <julius@bairaktaris.de>
---
--- a/nss_qdisc/igs/nss_ifb.c
+++ b/nss_qdisc/igs/nss_ifb.c
@@ -147,6 +147,18 @@
 	struct nss_ifb_info *ifb_info = (struct nss_ifb_info *)app_data;
 	bool ret;
 
+	/*
+	 * A CLEAR sent to unwind a half-applied mapping has no ifb_info to
+	 * update - there is no bound entry yet. Nothing to do but check the
+	 * response.
+	 */
+	if (!ifb_info) {
+		if (nim->cm.response != NSS_CMN_RESPONSE_ACK) {
+			nss_igs_error("Response error: %d\n", nim->cm.response);
+		}
+		return;
+	}
+
 	if (nim->cm.response != NSS_CMN_RESPONSE_ACK) {
 		nss_igs_error("Response error: %d\n", nim->cm.response);
 		return;
--- a/nss_qdisc/igs/nss_mirred.c
+++ b/nss_qdisc/igs/nss_mirred.c
@@ -191,6 +191,16 @@
 	 */
 	if (nss_ifb_config_msg_tx_sync(from_dev, ifb_num, NSS_IFB_SET_IGS_NODE, NULL) < 0) {
 		nss_igs_error("Sending config to %s dev failed\n", from_dev->name);
+
+		/*
+		 * A failure here is not proof the firmware ignored the message:
+		 * a timeout means only that no ACK was seen, and the node may
+		 * well be applied. is_mapped is set by nss_ifb_bind() below, so
+		 * from this point the gated undo helpers would return success
+		 * having sent nothing, and the node would sit on the physical
+		 * port until the firmware is reloaded. Undo it directly.
+		 */
+		nss_ifb_config_msg_tx(from_dev, ifb_num, NSS_IFB_CLEAR_IGS_NODE, NULL);
 		return -EINVAL;
 	}
 
@@ -199,6 +209,13 @@
 	 */
 	if (nss_ifb_config_msg_tx_sync(from_dev, ifb_num, NSS_IFB_SET_NEXTHOP, NULL) < 0) {
 		nss_igs_error("Sending next hop config to %s dev failed\n", from_dev->name);
+
+		/*
+		 * Same reasoning, and the IGS node from the send above is
+		 * definitely applied, so take both back.
+		 */
+		nss_ifb_config_msg_tx(from_dev, ifb_num, NSS_IFB_RESET_NEXTHOP, NULL);
+		nss_ifb_config_msg_tx(from_dev, ifb_num, NSS_IFB_CLEAR_IGS_NODE, NULL);
 		return -EINVAL;
 	}
 
